# BlinkySign configuration.
#
# Copy to .env and edit. install.sh does this for you.
#
# This file is read by systemd as well as by python-dotenv, and systemd is not
# a shell: use plain KEY=value lines, with no `export`, no $VAR expansion and no
# quoted values containing spaces.

# --- Web server ------------------------------------------------------------

BLINKYSIGN_PORT=5000
# BLINKYSIGN_HOST=0.0.0.0

# --- LEDs ------------------------------------------------------------------

BLINKYSIGN_LED_COUNT=30
BLINKYSIGN_LED_BRIGHTNESS=0.5

# auto tries SPI and falls back to a mock backend with a warning.
# Use spi to make a missing strip a hard failure, or mock to develop off-Pi.
# BLINKYSIGN_BACKEND=auto

# State colours. A name, #RRGGBB, or r,g,b.
# The default is green for muted, red for unmuted -- swap them if you prefer
# the opposite convention.
# BLINKYSIGN_MUTED_COLOR=green
# BLINKYSIGN_UNMUTED_COLOR=red

# --- Physical button -------------------------------------------------------

# BCM pin number. Leave unset if you have no button.
# BLINKYSIGN_BUTTON_PIN=17

# --- Discovery -------------------------------------------------------------

# Advertises http://blinkysign.local:5000 over mDNS.
# BLINKYSIGN_MDNS_ENABLED=true
# BLINKYSIGN_MDNS_NAME=blinkysign

# --- Access control --------------------------------------------------------

# Unset means the API is open to anyone on your network, which is the historic
# behaviour. Set a token to require x-api-key or Authorization: Bearer on every
# state-changing request.
# BLINKYSIGN_API_TOKEN=

# The panel is served by the sign itself, so it is same-origin and needs no
# CORS. Only set this if you are calling the API from another web app.
# BLINKYSIGN_CORS_ORIGINS=https://example.com

# --- Remote control over MQTT ----------------------------------------------
#
# Works with any broker. Some options:
#
#   Local Mosquitto:  HOST=localhost PORT=1883 TLS=none
#   HiveMQ / EMQX:    HOST=xxx.s1.eu.hivemq.cloud PORT=8883 TLS=server + user/pass
#   AWS IoT Core:     HOST=xxx-ats.iot.<region>.amazonaws.com PORT=8883 TLS=mutual
#                     with the device certificate and key
#
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_ENABLED=false
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_HOST=
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_PORT=1883
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_BASE_TOPIC=blinkysign
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_USERNAME=
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_PASSWORD=
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_TLS=none
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_CA_CERT=certs/AmazonRootCA1.pem
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_CERTFILE=certs/certificate.pem
# BLINKYSIGN_MQTT_KEYFILE=certs/private.key

# --- Home Assistant --------------------------------------------------------
#
# With MQTT enabled, the sign announces itself to Home Assistant automatically
# and appears as a device with a "Muted" switch and an "LEDs" light. Nothing to
# configure in Home Assistant beyond having the MQTT integration set up.
#
# BLINKYSIGN_HA_DISCOVERY=true
# BLINKYSIGN_HA_PREFIX=homeassistant
# BLINKYSIGN_HA_DEVICE_NAME=BlinkySign

# Note: there are deliberately no AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# entries here any more. The sign does not need AWS credentials, and keeping
# long-lived keys in a project file invites committing them.
